+Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. Committed changes reach connected browsers over self-hosted WebSockets, so updates arrive as they are committed without a realtime vendor and its recurring fees.
 
-Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery must be designed together after this choice. Polling would favor cursor queries; self-hosted sockets would need connection management and replay; hosted delivery would need publication reconciliation and provider credentials. Neither operating cost nor minimum operational burden has been declared the overriding criterion.
+Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery are designed together around this choice. The change store keeps replayable event revisions and publishes each committed revision reliably to the socket layer. Delivery manages socket sessions and fans revisions out to them. Browsers track the last revision they applied, reconnect with backoff when a socket drops, and catch up from that revision before resuming live updates. The project owns connection capacity and reconnect operations as ongoing work.
 
